HTTP Methods & Status Codes – Senior Developer Interview Guide

1. What is an HTTP Method?

An HTTP Method (Verb) defines the action that a client wants to perform on a resource in a REST API.

Example:

GET /users/10

Meaning: Retrieve user with ID 10.

POST /users

Meaning: Create a new user.

Think of HTTP methods as CRUD operations:

CRUD	HTTP Method

Create	POST
Read	GET
Update	PUT / PATCH
Delete	DELETE



---

2. Types of HTTP Methods

GET

Retrieve data.

GET /products
GET /products/101

Characteristics

Read-only

No data modification

Cacheable

Safe

Idempotent



---

POST

Create a new resource.

POST /users

Request Body:

{
  "name":"John"
}

Characteristics

Creates resource

Not idempotent

Not cacheable by default



---

PUT

Replace the entire resource.

PUT /users/1

{
  "name":"John",
  "email":"john@test.com"
}

Characteristics

Full update

Idempotent


Calling 10 times = same result.


---

PATCH

Partial update.

PATCH /users/1

{
  "email":"new@test.com"
}

Characteristics

Partial update

Usually idempotent



---

DELETE

Remove resource.

DELETE /users/1

Characteristics

Deletes resource

Idempotent


Deleting same user multiple times should produce same final state.


---

HEAD

Same as GET but returns headers only.

HEAD /products

Used for:

Check resource existence

Check file size

Health checks



---

OPTIONS

Returns supported methods.

OPTIONS /users

Response:

Allow: GET,POST,PUT,DELETE

Used heavily in:

CORS Preflight Requests



---

TRACE

Diagnostic method.

Echoes request back.

Rarely enabled because of security concerns.


---

CONNECT

Creates a tunnel connection.

Used by:

HTTPS Proxies

VPNs


Rare in application development.


---

3. Why Different HTTP Methods Exist?

Because each operation has a different meaning.

Bad:

POST /getUsers
POST /deleteUser
POST /updateUser

Good:

GET /users
DELETE /users/1
PUT /users/1
POST /users

Benefits:

Clarity

Easy to understand.

Caching

GET requests can be cached.

Security

Browsers understand safe operations.

REST Standards

Follows RESTful API design.

Tool Compatibility

Swagger, Postman, browsers, gateways all understand standard verbs.


---

4. HTTP Method Good Practices

Use GET only for Reading

❌ Wrong

GET /deleteUser/1

Can accidentally delete data.

✅ Correct

DELETE /users/1


---

Use POST for Creation

POST /orders

Not:

PUT /createOrder


---

Use PUT for Full Update

PUT /users/10

Entire resource is sent.


---

Use PATCH for Partial Update

PATCH /users/10

Update only changed fields.


---

Keep APIs Resource-Oriented

Bad:

POST /createProduct
POST /removeProduct

Good:

POST /products
DELETE /products/1


---

Return Correct Status Codes

Don't always return:

200 OK

Use meaningful codes.


---

Design Idempotent APIs

Good:

PUT /users/1

Calling multiple times should not create duplicates.


---

Validate Request Body

Return:

400 Bad Request

for invalid input.


---

HTTP Status Codes

1xx Informational

Rarely used.

100 Continue
101 Switching Protocols


---

2xx Success

200 OK

Request successful.

GET /users


---

201 Created

Resource created.

POST /users


---

202 Accepted

Request accepted for background processing.

Example:

POST /report-generation


---

204 No Content

Success but no response body.

DELETE /users/1


---

3xx Redirection

301 Moved Permanently

Permanent redirect.


---

302 Found

Temporary redirect.


---

304 Not Modified

Browser cache still valid.


---

4xx Client Errors

400 Bad Request

Invalid request.


---

401 Unauthorized

Authentication required.


---

403 Forbidden

Authenticated but access denied.


---

404 Not Found

Resource doesn't exist.


---

