3. Content Negotiation

Client can ask for specific response format.

Request:

Accept: application/json

or

Accept: application/xml

Server responds accordingly.


---

4. Important Request Headers

Authorization

Authorization: Bearer JWT_TOKEN

Authentication.


---

Content-Type

Content-Type: application/json

Tells server request body format.


---

Accept

Accept: application/json

Expected response format.


---

Origin

Used in CORS.

Origin: https://example.com


---

5. Important Response Headers

Cache-Control

Cache-Control: max-age=3600

Cache for 1 hour.


---

Location

After creation:

HTTP/1.1 201 Created
Location: /users/101

Senior interview favorite.


---

Access-Control-Allow-Origin

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

CORS.


---

6. Conditional Requests

Reduce unnecessary downloads.

Browser sends:

If-Modified-Since

or

If-None-Match

Server returns:

304 Not Modified

No need to resend data.


---

7. ETag

Resource fingerprint/version.

Response:

ETag: "abc123"

Later:

If-None-Match: "abc123"

If unchanged:

304 Not Modified

Very common in scalable systems.


---

8. Rate Limiting

Protect APIs.

Response:

429 Too Many Requests

Headers:

X-RateLimit-Limit
X-RateLimit-Remaining

Example:

100 requests/minute



---

9. API Versioning

URL Versioning

/api/v1/users
/api/v2/users

Most common.


---

Header Versioning

Accept: application/vnd.company.v2+json

Less common.

Interview Question:

> How do you release breaking API changes?



Answer:

> API versioning.




